/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - IP cores are classified by what the supplier delivers and how much the buyer can change.
   - Hard IP arrives as a mask-level layout, often GDSII.
   - Firm IP arrives as a gate-level netlist or a characterized RTL structure for a selected process.
   - Soft IP arrives as synthesizable register-transfer level (RTL) code, usually Verilog or VHDL.
   - The license matters.
   - Integration starts with a wrapper.
   - The core connects to an on-chip bus.
   - Every core with registers or memory that software reads or writes needs an address range in the memory map; a PLL or a purely streaming datapath may have none.
   - Clock and reset are next.
   - Power domains add isolation cells, level shifters and retention.
*/
